00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.969 align:start position:0%
 
when you talk about the progress that  you're making and the changes that  you're seeing happen does it worry you  that this kind of progress will be held  up or held into question as you look at  this political cycle when we have  candidates who are so outspoken or  negative in terms of foreign policy  countries like China when Donald Trump  is so negative does it concern you in  terms of the good progress you've made  well I won't weigh in on politics become  diplomatic enough to say very clear of  that but I will say that you know  certain aspects of foreign policy have  to have continuity there is no way to  deal with the threats of our time to  deal with ISIL to stop the financing  without actually building multilateral  coalition's and to build multilateral  coalition's you have to hear and listen  what other countries interests are you  have to be capable of building bridges  and I think President Obama has done a  forceful job of that we have a 65  country coalition against ISIL we have a  coalition to combat climate change we've  invested in UN peacekeeping which goes  to places that are have not yes sir yet  necessarily gone to full-fledged war  that implicates our direct national  interest in the way that that's  traditionally conceived but if we nip  things in the bud that might mean fewer  extremists in the long term so you got  to invest in the in the multilateral  institutions in the multilateral  framework you can't just show up and get  what you want when you want it another  subject we talk about every day in this  program is Europe there are a lot of  headwinds that Europe's facing right now  and some of them are coming from the  refugee the migrant situation and even  overnight there was talk about a  possible deal with Turkey and the EU you  have been very involved in this issue  where are we right now in terms of  refugees coming out of Syria in  particular but Middle East more  generally and what's the path forward  well first to say this is a global  phenomenon a global tragedy there's 60  million displaced people 20 million of  whom are refugees meaning they cross an  international border the world has never  been more generous private donors have  never been more generous governments and  yet the need is so outpacing the demand  one of the reasons we saw the spike in  the flow to Europe was that the World  Food Programme because of the size of  the appeal and again the inability to  fill it you despite the u.s. giving five  billion dollars on the to the Syria  humanitarian appeals over the life of  the war there but by virtue of the fact  that  were being cut schools were not being  set up because there just was barely  enough resources to feed people never  mind to take on the schooling that's  when people began to vote with their  feet and they began that they got text  messages saying your rations are going  to be cut in half if you're a parent  you're looking at your kids you're  thinking okay now make or break let me  entrust my family's life to the smuggler  that might be the best I can do it so  it's either barrel bombs from the Assad  regime ISIL in this area or a smuggler  it's it's talk about Sophie's choices so  I think what's important is that just as  the the phenomenon is a global  phenomenon so to the responsibility to  deal with it has to be spread more  equitably around the world and President  Obama actually in September at the  General Assembly when the heads of state  come back together is going to be  chairing a head of state summit to try  to actually spread the burden across  more countries there many countries in  the world who don't take refugees at all  they may be geographically far afield  from Syria but there's a way to actually  spread the burden the right now is  falling on the neighboring countries  first and foremost turkey Jordan Lebanon  principally but now of course into  Europe but the sonic state what you're  talking about is the symptom the  refugees are the symptom of a  fundamental underlying disease yes can  we really solve this without radical  regime change in syria itself well what  we what we can't solve it with in order  to solve it we have to have political  solutions and that's why secretary Kerry  is investing so much including in  collaboration with the Russian  Federation which has been on a very  different side of this conflict with a  better part of five years but we were we  are recognizing that the cut would that  one of the reasons the refugee flow is  what it is is that conflicts aren't  ending as quickly as they once did and  so Syria is a good example it's five  years and counting we've started to see  a reduction in the violence by virtue of  a cessation of hostilities that's  important there's less refugee flow when  there are fewer bombs falling from the  sky but a long term solution is going to  require something that's going to  convince the majority of Syrians to put  their guns down and convince Syrians  abroad when the time is right to  actually come home and that's going to  take a lot more work so realistically  how close are we to any sort of real  resolution to stop this i mean there's  meetings going on in Turkey right now  saying will there be a resolution is  that even a  possibility as we head into warmer  months we're only going to see more  refugees cross into countries like  Greece and Italy I think that's where  David's point is so important we've got  to get a political solution not just for  Syria we need out in Myanmar there's the  same issue of rahunga getting on boats  and risking their lives and interesting  smugglers and so forth again the rights  within a country dictate whether or not  people are willing to entrust the lives  of their families to frankly horrible  horrible people who are putting innocent  families on dinghies that barely float  and giving them life jackets that don't  function and so how close are we I mean  it really depends on what we can achieve  again on the political track in terms of  whether the flow will continue but I  think it's also important to note that  Turkey Jordan Lebanon you know their  financial investments we can make in  these areas the host communities that  have sheltered these refugees for more  than now going on five years i have  really born this burden without it being  spread around the world and so part of  what has to happen is we the World Bank  others need to invest more again and  those and those who shouldered a  disproportionate burden it strikes me  that we have the situation in Libya  where the the world the Western world  took a very different approach fairly  militarily active approach and the US  had a very significant orla and we end  up with a result that wasn't very good  what you've been a student at this for  some years what have we learned from the  experiences of Syria versus Libya about  what the best world response is well of  course not we can't be satisfied with  either outcome the situation in Libya is  very unstable although they're moving  haltingly toward a kind of government of  national Accord which couldn't come a  moment too soon because ISIL has laid  down a foothold there look in Libya  there was a great reluctance on the part  of the Libyans who had earned themselves  life without Qaddafi to want an  international presence of any kind on  the ground very proud very attendant to  their sovereignty and basically said  thank you for the help getting rid of  Gaddafi we got it from here and then  there were divisions that that toured  the society apart in Syria it's very  hard to argue i think when one looks  back at the five years no one can be  proud of the results because of the  heartbreak that  fence every day because of the rise of  terrorism but by the same token it's  hard to see this magic tool that had we  just done X or Y suddenly you know the  the demons that have been unleashed in  Syria would have gone back into the into  the box or into the bottle so it's these  are very complex societies we open the  tool tool box that we have we try to  deploy as many tools as we can to  mitigate suffering in order to get the  kinds of political solutions that will  bring conflicts to an end sooner but it  but it's challenging and we only have a  short time left as the Obama  administration we're going to work every  minute to try to put those conflicts in  a much better place before we leave  we're not going to talk politics but as  you watch all of this unfold foreign  policy is going to be extraordinarily  important and difficult for the next  president of the United States may be  more difficult than any president we  will see in our lifetime what is the  most important thing that needs to be  employed those first hundred days when  you look at this what do you think we  need the most well I think an investment  in diplomacy a lot there's a lot of talk  in the certain circles in the campaign  about military force military force  where would we use it what would we do  but truly building these coalition's is  what is required to bring about  political solutions and there's not one  of the conflicts on the global stage  that can be settled simply by the barrel  of the gun even the terrorism challenge  where we've invested of course in  combating that militarily in building  this coalition you've got to cut off  financing you've got to maintain border  security you've got to share information  about who's who in order to protect you  know your people and make sure the wrong  people don't come into your country then  are you surprised that diplomacy is I  feel like what we've heard the least of  in all of these debates on all of these  meetings what's not what is it I don't  know I'm a diplomatic nerd but it  doesn't make much of a soundbite it's  not like the bumper sticker don't go do  diplomacy you know go listen that's not  going to work necessarily in a campaign  but but one hopes that cooler heads will  prevail and you know there's there there  are a lot of career foreign policy  professionals who are also fuel our  foreign policy across administration's  regardless of who's in place so again we  hope that investing the kinds of  coalition's that you need understanding  that  military force can be a component of  dealing with violent people especially  but it but it has to be part of a  fulsome strategy I mean that's something  that because you can't succeed otherwise  ultimately people are going to come to  that conclusion self-described  diplomatic nerve right there you said  there's no there's no sound bites around  diplomacy that's a bumper sticker but  we're not going to let you have it this  woman is some extraordinary guest  Ambassador Samantha power thank you so  much for joining us today

